  <h2>1. Find the themes before you write</h2>
  <p>
    Read your notes, not the papers, and sort them into groups by the question each paper answers or the approach it
    takes. Three to five themes usually cover a contribution. Papers can sit in more than one.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Write each theme as an argument</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open with what the group of papers established.</li>
    <li>Compare them: where they agree, differ, and why.</li>
    <li>Close with the limitation that your work addresses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Check every claim against its source</h2>
  <p>
    “X showed Y” is a factual claim. Open the passage and confirm it says Y, in the conditions you imply. Notes that link
    to their passage make this fast; summaries from an AI make it essential, since a
    <a href={href('glossary/hallucination/')}>hallucination</a> can put a claim in the wrong paper.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Don't miss the obvious paper</h2>
  <p>
    Papers that many of your sources cite, their <a href={href('glossary/co-citation/')}>co-cited</a> core, are the ones
    reviewers expect. Check the references of your key papers and the papers citing them.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One paragraph per paper, in chronological order.</li>
    <li>Describing papers without saying how they relate to yours.</li>
    <li>Citing a paper for a claim it doesn't make.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
